2186045417] [17592186501124 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501124 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501124 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501124 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501124 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501124 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501124 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501124 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186500638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501124 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e953d84f-9d70-56d1-a2e6-25501f223dc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501144 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501144 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79310" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501144 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501144 #edb/ref 1071 "A cyclist has a small puncture in one of her tires. The volume of air $V,$ in centimeters cubed, in the tire decreases at a rate that's proportional to the volume at that moment. Every day, the cyclist pumps $10\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ back into the tire.\nWhat is the differential equation describing the rate of change of the volume of air in the tire  at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time in days?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501144 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1800d774-9d7c-5ba7-a1e5-da93acbae2ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501144 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate of change in the volume of air per day decreases in proportion to $V$ and increases by $12.$ The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV+10,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the (positive) constant of proportionality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501160 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501160 #edb/ref 1069 "q-119185" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501160 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501160 #edb/ref 1071 "An oil tank has a leak. The rate of change of the volume $V$ (in liters) of oil in the tank is directly proportional to the time $t$ (in minutes) passed since the tank started leaking. However, the tank is also refilled with $4$ liters of oil every minute. What is the differential equation that describes the rate of change of the volume of oil in the tank at time $t?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501160 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3d4170a5-9522-5ae2-b33d-b3d78a6d10b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501160 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate of change in the oil volume decreases in proportion to time $t$ and increases by $4$ liters per minute. The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kt+4,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the (positive) constant of proportionality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501179 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501179 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143109" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501179 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501179 #edb/ref 1071 "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis. What differential equation describes this curve?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501179 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "70f26979-0b45-521e-afad-0123af31ff5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501179 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *shortest distance from $P(x,y)$ to the $y$-axis * is given by $|x|.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional* to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\cdot |x|,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k|x|.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501187 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501187 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143110" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501187 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501187 #edb/ref 1071 "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the square of the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis. What differential equation describes this curve?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501187 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7fc67f3e-fcb8-5740-a171-eb4e59b85b29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501187 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **square of the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *square of the shortest distance from $P(x,y)$ to the $x$-axis* is given by ${y}^{2}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional* to the shortest distance from $P(x,y)$ to the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\cdot {y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k{y}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501189 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501189 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143130" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501189 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501189 #edb/ref 1071 "The area of a circle increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of its circumference. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501189 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "842c8f8f-382b-5ffe-9458-677d53445332" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501189 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a circle **increases** at a **rate** that's **inversely proportional** to **the square of its circumference**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate that's *inversely proportional* to *the square of its circumference.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{(2\\pi r)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{(2\\pi r)^{2}} \\\\ 2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{4{\\pi }^{2}{r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{8{\\pi }^{3}{r}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{8{\\pi }^{3}}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{r}^{3}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501193 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501193 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143134" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501193 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501193 #edb/ref 1071 "The area of a square increases at a rate proportional to the cube of its perimeter. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the side length, $s(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501193 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a543fb6-3a0e-5397-9156-31fc05eeae07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501193 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a square **increases** at a rate **proportional** to the **cube of its perimeter**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a square* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate *proportional to the cube of its perimeter*. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k(4s)^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a square is given by \n\n$$\nA={s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({s}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =k(4s)^{3} \\\\ 2s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =64k{s}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{64k{s}^{3}}{2s} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =32k{s}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=32k>0\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=K{s}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501195 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501195 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143107" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501195 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501195 #edb/ref 1071 "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the squared distance from $P$ to the origin. What differential equation describes this curve?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501195 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "94332050-ff41-5fa8-9cce-c854bd7e0d1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501195 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **squared distance from $P$ to the origin**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *squared distance from $P(x,y)$ to the origin* is given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional* to the squared distance from $P$ to the origin, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\cdot ({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501197 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501197 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143142" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501197 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501197 #edb/ref 1071 "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis. The derivative at the point $(-3,6)$ equals $12.$ What differential equation describes this curve?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501197 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9801499d-e8ba-50b1-a83f-440b9081ac28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501197 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis* is given by $|y|.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional to* the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\cdot |y|\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k|y|.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{′}(x)=12$ when at the point $(-3,6).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12=k|6|\\,⟹\\,k=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=2|y|.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501199 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501199 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501199 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501199 #edb/ref 1071 "The volume of a sphere increases at a rate proportional to the square of its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501199 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9e12d746-15a9-5bf9-9bc2-9113fc7fbbef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501199 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a sphere **increases** at a rate **proportional** to the **square of its radius**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate *proportional to the square of its radius.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =k{r}^{2} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k{r}^{2}}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{4\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{4\\pi }>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501204 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501204 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66523" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501204 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501204 #edb/ref 1071 "Tom's basketball has a hole in it, meaning its volume decreases at a rate that's proportional to its volume. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ of a ball. Assume that $K>0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501204 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a8090d53-1681-5d0f-a69a-1950471f0a95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501204 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of the ball **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **volume**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to its volume.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-kV \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{(\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{3})}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{12\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{kr}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{3}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501219 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501219 #edb/ref 1069 "q-144820" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501219 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501219 #edb/ref 1071 "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin. The derivative at the point $(2,-4)$ equals $12.$ What differential equation describes this curve?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501219 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bb3732e8-5921-5cbd-a1fe-55832a8ffa5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501219 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *slope of the line through $P(x,y)$ and the origin* is given by $\\frac{y}{x}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional to* the square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\cdot {(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{k{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{′}(x)=12$ when at the point $(2,-4).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12=\\frac{k(-4)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{12}{4}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
